7. LAYER 3 — ARIA INTELLIGENCE ENGINE
Aria Powered shall operate as a collection of coordinated AI capabilities rather than a single unrestricted conversational process.
The Aria layer shall include:
Intent Interpreter
Specification Parser
Architectural Reasoning Engine
Constraint Reasoner
Code Reasoning Interface
Spatial Reasoner
Design Generator
Revision Interpreter
Conflict Explanation Engine
Documentation Planner
Quality-Control Agent
Project Orchestrator
These components may use one or more underlying AI models.
The customer should experience them as one coherent intelligence.

8. ARIA ORCHESTRATOR
The Aria Orchestrator is the central intelligence coordinator.
It determines which operation is required.
For example:
Customer:
“Move the kitchen 1.5 metres east and maintain the existing exterior envelope.”
Aria Orchestrator determines:
Intent: Modify spatial configuration.
Then identifies:
Affected Space: Kitchen
Affected Geometry: Interior partitions
Constraint: Exterior envelope immutable
Potential Dependencies: Plumbing, electrical, cabinetry, windows, circulation
The orchestrator then submits the operation to the appropriate computational services.
Aria does not simply return prose.
It initiates a structured model operation.

9. STRUCTURED AI OPERATIONS
AI actions shall be represented as structured commands.
Conceptually:
CREATE_SPACE
MOVE_ELEMENT
ROTATE_ELEMENT
RESIZE_SPACE
ADD_LEVEL
CHANGE_ASSEMBLY
ADD_DOOR
ADD_WINDOW
MODIFY_REQUIREMENT
GENERATE_DRAWING
RUN_VALIDATION
CREATE_REDLINE
RESOLVE_REDLINE
REGENERATE_DOCUMENT_SET
The AI layer should therefore communicate with the Project Model through controlled operations rather than unrestricted database modification.

10. AI GUARDRAIL
Aria shall never directly manipulate production database records without passing through the model-command layer.
The operation becomes:
AI INTENT
↓
STRUCTURED COMMAND
↓
COMMAND VALIDATION
↓
PROJECT MODEL TRANSACTION
↓
GEOMETRY UPDATE
↓
VALIDATION
↓
COMMIT
This creates a transaction boundary around AI activity.
If validation fails, the transaction may be rejected or placed into a review state.

11. PROJECT ORCHESTRATOR
The Project Orchestrator manages multi-stage operations.
For example:
A customer changes the building footprint.
The orchestrator can determine that this requires:
Site impact analysis
Spatial regeneration
Structural coordination
Code validation
Dimension regeneration
Elevation regeneration
Section regeneration
Schedule updates
Drawing regeneration
The customer should experience this as one operation:
Updating Building Footprint…
rather than manually executing nine different processes.

12. LAYER 4 — PROJECT MODEL SERVICE
The Project Model Service owns the canonical project state.
It manages:
Project Objects
Relationships
Constraints
Versions
Dependencies
Model Transactions
Snapshots
Revision State
The service exposes controlled interfaces to other systems.

13. DATABASE ARCHITECTURE
OneDraft should use multiple persistence mechanisms rather than forcing every type of information into one database.
The architecture should distinguish:
Transactional Data
Geometric Data
Document Data
Search/Index Data
Object Storage
Audit Data
This allows each workload to use an appropriate storage mechanism.

