Object storage records shall be associated with Project_ID and Version_ID.

16. MODEL VERSIONING
The project model shall support versioned state.
Conceptually:
PROJECT-001
MODEL-001
↓
MODEL-002
↓
MODEL-003
Every model revision must be reconstructable.
This allows OneDraft to answer:
What did the project look like before this redline?
and:
Which geometry produced this drawing?

17. EVENT LOG
Major project events shall generate immutable events.
Examples:
PROJECT_CREATED
SPECIFICATION_SUBMITTED
CODE_MANIFEST_CREATED
MODEL_GENERATED
ELEMENT_MODIFIED
VALIDATION_COMPLETED
REDLINE_CREATED
REDLINE_RESOLVED
DRAWINGS_GENERATED
CUSTOMER_ACCEPTED
DOCUMENT_EXPORTED
The event stream becomes a second layer of project provenance.

18. LAYER 5 — CAD GEOMETRY ENGINE
The Geometry Engine is responsible for converting model instructions into actual computational geometry.
It shall support:
2D CAD Geometry
3D Geometry
Parametric Geometry
Boolean Operations
Spatial Relationships
Coordinate Systems
Constraints
Assemblies
Views
Sections
Projections
Dimension Extraction
The engine is independent of Aria.
Aria instructs it.
The Geometry Engine performs the computation.

19. CAD REPRESENTATION
The internal model should maintain semantic objects rather than merely linework.
For example:
A wall should be:
Wall Object
rather than:
Four Lines
A door should be:
Door Object
rather than:
Two Arcs + Rectangle
A stair should be:
Stair Object
rather than:
Repeated Lines
This permits the system to understand what it is drawing.

20. PARAMETRIC MODEL
Elements shall possess parameters.
Example:
WALL-00427
Parameters:
Length
Height
Thickness
Assembly
Location
Fire Rating
Material
Acoustic Rating
Thermal Properties
Changing a parameter triggers dependency evaluation.

21. DEPENDENCY GRAPH
The Project Model shall maintain a dependency graph.
For example:
WALL-00427
affects:
ROOM-003
which affects:
DIM-011
which affects:
PLAN-A121
and:
SECTION-A401
and:
ELEVATION-A301
The system can therefore determine the minimum set of outputs requiring regeneration.
This is a critical optimization.
One small change should not require blindly rebuilding the entire project.

22. DOCUMENTATION ENGINE
The Documentation Engine converts model information into architectural drawings.
Its responsibilities include:
View Creation
Linework Generation
Dimension Generation
Annotation
Tags
Schedules
Detail References
Section References
Sheet Composition
Title Blocks
Revision Blocks
Drawing Index
Graphic Standards

23. ARCH D OUTPUT
OneDraft shall support ARCH D sheet generation as a first-class document format.
The system should control:
Page Dimensions
Margins
Title Block
Drawing Scale
Line Weight
Text Hierarchy
Dimension Style
Graphic Symbols
North Arrow
Graphic Scale
Sheet Number
Revision Block
Project Information
The visual objective is professional architectural documentation rather than an AI-generated illustration.

24. RENDERING ENGINE
A separate Rendering Engine shall generate visual representations of the model.
Rendering modes may include:
Technical CAD
Blue-on-White Drawing
Axonometric
Architectural Visualization
Photorealistic
Conceptual
The renderer shall operate upon the same underlying model.
Thus:
CAD drawing
and:
architectural rendering
remain different representations of the same building.

25. SECTION ENGINE
Sections shall be computationally generated from the model.
The system identifies:
Section Plane
↓
Intersecting Geometry
↓
Cut Geometry
↓
Projection Geometry
↓
Annotations
↓
Dimensions
↓
Section Drawing
This prevents the common failure where a section visually resembles the building but does not correspond to its floor plans.

26. ELEVATION ENGINE
Elevations shall similarly be generated from model geometry.
An elevation shall be a projection of the actual building.
Changes to:
Windows
Doors
Roof Geometry
Wall Heights
Materials
Masses
should automatically affect the corresponding elevation.

27. SCHEDULE ENGINE
Schedules shall be generated from model objects.
Examples:
Door Schedule
Window Schedule
Room Schedule
Finish Schedule
Equipment Schedule
The schedule engine shall query the Project Model rather than rely on manually duplicated information.

28. LAYER 6 — REGULATORY INTELLIGENCE
The Code Intelligence Service shall operate separately from the architectural reasoning engine.
Its responsibilities include:
Jurisdiction Identification
Regulatory Source Identification
Version Identification
Effective-Date Determination
Rule Extraction
Applicability Analysis
Code Manifest Generation
Rule Verification
Change Detection
This separation is intentional.
Architectural reasoning should not silently invent regulatory requirements.

29. REGULATORY SOURCE PIPELINE
The regulatory pipeline is:
JURISDICTION
↓
SOURCE DISCOVERY
↓
SOURCE RETRIEVAL
↓
VERSION IDENTIFICATION
↓
DOCUMENT NORMALIZATION
↓
RULE EXTRACTION
↓
APPLICABILITY MODEL
↓
CODE MANIFEST
↓
PROJECT VALIDATION
The system should retain source provenance.

30. CODE HASHING / PROVENANCE
The regulatory baseline shall generate a cryptographic identifier.
Conceptually:
HASH(Source_ID + Version + Effective_Date + Retrieved_Content + Jurisdiction)
The resulting manifest becomes associated with the project model.
If the regulatory dataset changes, the system creates a new manifest rather than silently replacing the old one.

31. VALIDATION ENGINE
The Validation Engine evaluates the project against:
Customer Requirements
Geometry
Spatial Rules
Code Rules
Accessibility
Egress
Life Safety
Zoning
Internal Coordination
Drawing Coordination
The engine returns machine-readable results.
Example:
RULE-0047
STATUS: REVIEW_REQUIRED
AFFECTED ELEMENT: STAIR-002
REASON: Required condition requires professional verification.
This result can then appear in the user interface.

32. HUMAN REVIEW BOUNDARY
OneDraft shall distinguish between computational verification and professional certification.
The system may state:
“Model condition satisfies the configured computational rule.”
It should not automatically transform that statement into:
“Municipally approved.”
Similarly:
“Drawing generated.”
is different from:
“Drawing professionally sealed.”
and:
“Permit approved.”
The system maintains these states independently.

33. LAYER 7 — DOCUMENT PRODUCTION
